    "Coefficients set relative importance only; direction comes from higher_is_better. Each metric's improvement over the stored reference (scoring/scores_test.json, method spmc_frs) is normalised by its spread across ranked attempts.",
    "No metric depends on wall-clock time. The paper's speed claim (< 1 s per case, O(n)) is enforced as a constraint instead: the evaluator caps every method at 1 CPU-second per instance (kernel-measured, ~20x the reference); exceeding it fails the run.",
    "A failed run (crash, invalid/non-SO(3) output, import failure, CPU budget overrun) receives the worst value of every metric: 180 deg for error metrics, 0 for success rates.",
    "results.* for the two robust_alignment experiments: median of the paper's printed per-(pattern, configuration) medians in Fig. 4 (35 configurations for _all, the 15 B5-B7 ones for _high_outliers); the paper prints no pooled median, so this is the closest paper-derived aggregate of the gym's pooled median. The full Fig. 4 transcription is under _paper_reported_unscored.fig4_medians_deg; earlier unverified aggregates are kept under _paper_reported_unscored.earlier_unverified_aggregates. sparse_alignment has no paper counterpart: its results are this gym's measured reference (hidden test).",
    "Image experiments use a public world-map substitute (the paper's image is unreleased) with synthetic clutter at the paper's five clutter levels, so the paper's 0.89/1.32/2.96 deg values are indicative, not same-setup targets."
